<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Gwiriko</h1><p class="st-meta">1714 CE – 1897 CE (approx.) · capital Bobo-Dioulasso</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Dyula-ruled kingdom around Bobo-Dioulasso in western Burkina Faso, founded by a branch of the Wattara family of Kong.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Gwiriko was created around 1714 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/famagan-wattara">Famagan Wattara</a>, a kinsman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seku-wattara">Seku Wattara</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kong-empire">Kong Empire</a>, who led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> warriors into the region of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-volta">Black Volta</a>. He established his rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobo-people">Bobo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bwa-people">Bwa</a> and other farming peoples, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobo-dioulasso">Sya</a>, later Bobo-Dioulasso, as the main centre. After Seku's death it became independent of Kong.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Wattara and their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> allies formed a Muslim ruling and trading class over largely non-Muslim farming villages, collecting tribute and controlling trade in kola, salt, gold and slaves. After Famagan's reign the kingdom fragmented into rival branches, and in the 19th century it was weakened by revolts of subject peoples and wars with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kenedougou-kingdom">Kenedougou</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>By the 1890s Gwiriko was a loose collection of chiefdoms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Toure</a>'s armies passed through the region, and in 1897 the French occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobo-dioulasso">Bobo-Dioulasso</a>. The area became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-sudan">French Sudan</a> and later <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-upper-volta">French Upper Volta</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1714 CE – 1897 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:93.81%;width:3.64%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1714 CE – 1897 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bobo-Dioulasso</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Famagan Wattara</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam and traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dyula, Bobo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gwiriko</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1806&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gwiriko" aria-label="Kingdom of Gwiriko on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4562 1760 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Gwiriko lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4882 2016L4873 2092M4882 2016L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4873 2092h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4882 2016h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4882 2016h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bobo-Dioulasso. <a href="/atlas?year=1806&amp;focus=kingdom-of-gwiriko">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
